    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i see. so with /s/ it is just air, right? it sounds like a hiss. and /z/ has more of a buzz or a hum to it? i'm trying to understand the physical mechanics better, like what exactly is happening in the throat to make that noise change.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's correct; /s/ is purely air expelled, creating a hissing friction sound. For /z/, the airflow is vibrating the vocal cords, which adds a distinct, audible hum or buzz. Physically, the difference lies in the tension and pulsing of the vocal folds in your larynx. When voiced, the folds come together and vibrate rapidly, much like a vibrating string.",
      "source": "target",
