    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "so the cords basically act like a valve or something? i'm a bit confused about how the sound goes from just a hiss to a buzz. is it like a specific muscle movement that turns the vibration on and off?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of them less like a simple valve and more like a controlled, vibrating reed. In voicing, muscles adjust to bring the vocal cords close enough to intersect and oscillate consistently. This sustained vibration of the cords is what generates the low-frequency buzz that we perceive as voice. Switching between the two is mostly a matter of controlling the tension and contact between these folds.",
